      en: "The Aweil Rice Scheme is an irrigation project in Northern Bahr el Ghazal State, South Sudan, initiated in the 1970s with USAID funding. It covers 10,000 hectares along the Lol River, aiming to boost rice production for food security. At its peak in the 1980s, it produced 20,000 tons annually. The civil war destroyed canals, pumps, and infrastructure. After 2005, rehabilitation efforts restored partial capacity, but production remains below 5% of potential. Soil salinization and lack of maintenance are major challenges. The scheme employs thousands of local workers during harvest. In 2018, the government signed a deal with a Chinese firm to rehabilitate the project. It is considered a strategic asset for South Sudan's agricultural development.",
